The payback period is the time it takes for the cumulative cash inflows to equal the initial investment.

\[
\text{Payback period} = \frac{\text{Initial investment}}{\text{Annual cash inflow}}
= \frac{200{,}000}{60{,}000}
= 3.\overline{3}\text{ years}
\]

So the payback period is **approximately 3.33 years** (about 3 years and 4 months).

The earnings per share (EPS) are:

\[
\text{EPS} = \frac{\text{Net Income}}{\text{Shares Outstanding}}
           = \frac{25{,}000{,}000}{10{,}000{,}000}
           = 2.5 \text{ rupees per share}
\]

With a P/E ratio of 18, the share price \(P\) is:

\[
P = \text{EPS} \times \text{P/E}
   = 2.5 \times 18
   = 45 \text{ rupees}
\]

**Current share price: ₹45 per share.**
